// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`MonteCarloMethodologyModal > renders a stable size="3xl" dialog snapshot (no overflow regression) 1`] = `
<section
  aria-labelledby="react-aria-id-1"
  class="flex min-h-0 flex-1 flex-col outline-none"
  data-rac=""
  role="dialog"
  tabindex="-1"
>
  <div
    class="flex min-h-0 flex-1 flex-col"
  >
    <h2
      class="mt-0 mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-ink shrink-0"
      id="react-aria-id-1"
      slot="title"
    >
      How success probability is calculated
    </h2>
    <div
      aria-label="Success probability methodology details"
      class="min-h-0 flex-1 overflow-y-auto -mx-2 px-2 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:-outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-ring"
      role="region"
      tabindex="0"
    >
      <p
        class="text-sm text-muted"
      >
        The success probability is the share of 10,000 simulated retirement futures in which your portfolio still has money at the end of your plan horizon. A score of 85% means 8,500 of those futures ended with a positive balance.
      </p>
      <h3
        class="mt-4 mb-1 text-sm font-semibold text-ink"
      >
        What varies across simulations
      </h3>
      <p
        class="text-sm text-muted"
      >
        Each simulation draws a different sequence of historical monthly returns, equity and bond returns are sampled from the same historical month so their correlation is preserved. Inflation (CPI) is sampled from the same historical record and applied to your living expenses, so a run with high equity returns can still pair with high inflation.
      </p>
      <h3
        class="mt-4 mb-1 text-sm font-semibold text-ink"
      >
        What stays the same across simulations
      </h3>
      <p
        class="text-sm text-muted"
      >
        Most income amounts are pre-computed from your scenario settings and stay the same across simulations. Pension, annuity, and part-time income each carry a
         
        <em>
          Tracks scenario inflation
        </em>
         
        toggle: when off, the stream grows at the explicit COLA rate you set (e.g. 0% for a fixed pension). When on — and for Social Security in every scenario — the stream is adjusted to each path's realized inflation, so it varies slightly across simulations when that path's CPI departs from the scenario's expected rate.
      </p>
      <h3
        class="mt-4 mb-1 text-sm font-semibold text-ink"
      >
        Limitations
      </h3>
      <p
        class="text-sm text-muted"
      >
        Past market sequences are not a guarantee of future returns. Taxes are modeled in each path: the federal income tax, Medicare IRMAA, and ACA premium-tax-credit cashflow for that year is funded from the portfolio, so it lowers balances and can move the depletion year. That tax estimate is a single-pass approximation, not an exact filing: it does not re-solve withdrawals and tax together within a year, it routes each year's withdrawal by account tax treatment (pre-tax, Roth, taxable, cash-like) rather than re-optimizing the draw order, it computes each year's required minimum distribution (RMD) floor from each owner's own pre-tax (tax-deferred) account balances (so an RMD-age household is taxed on the full required minimum even when actual draws are lower, while a Roth-only or all-taxable household has no RMD floor and sees no phantom required income), and it funds the full IRMAA surcharge from the portfolio — choices that lean conservative (slightly higher tax). The model also does not capture sequence-of-returns risk specific to your drawdown order.
      </p>
      <p
        class="text-sm text-muted"
      >
        Simulated market histories are not a forecast. Past performance does not predict what your accounts will earn.
      </p>
      <p
        class="mt-3 text-sm font-medium text-ink"
      >
        What this stress test does and does not model
      </p>
      <ul
        class="mt-2 ml-5 list-disc text-sm text-muted"
      >
        <li>
          <strong
            class="font-semibold"
          >
            Spending guardrails are modeled when you turn them on.
          </strong>
           
          With guardrails enabled, spending is cut after a bad stretch and restored after a good one inside every simulated path, and the success probability shown is the one with guardrails applied. With guardrails off, spending never reacts to a downturn.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong
            class="font-semibold"
          >
            10,000 paths carry their own sampling error.
          </strong>
           
          The success probability is an estimate from a finite sample, so re-running the same unchanged plan with a fresh sample typically moves it by well under a percentage point. Treat one-point differences between plans as noise, not signal.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong
            class="font-semibold"
          >
            Medical costs rise faster, but not more erratically.
          </strong>
           
          Expenses you flag as medical grow about 2 percentage points a year faster than general inflation, in every path. There is no separate medical-inflation surprise on top of that: medical costs move in lockstep with general inflation here, so the very worst outcomes for a medical-heavy household are understated.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong
            class="font-semibold"
          >
            The longer-life comparison stretches the plan, it does not rebuild it.
          </strong>
           
          When you compare against a longer life, the added years match the base run only up to the original end of your plan — past that point they use fresh market draws. In those added years Social Security and other income repeat their last dollar amount with no further cost-of-living increase, so their buying power falls, and the plan keeps whichever tax return it was already filing rather than working out a new one. Together these make the added years an estimate rather than an exact re-run: the repeated income buys less over time, while keeping the earlier tax return and drawing fresh market years can each move the result in either direction.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong
            class="font-semibold"
          >
            Clustered history, monthly-average prices.
          </strong>
           
          Each simulated future is built from consecutive multi-month stretches of real history sampled together, so booms and busts cluster into the kind of sustained good and bad streaks markets actually have. The underlying Shiller series still uses monthly-average prices, which understate true month-to-month swings by roughly 10–20%, so this stress test slightly understates downside volatility.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong
            class="font-semibold"
          >
            Inflation framing trims real returns.
          </strong>
           
          Inflation is mean-centered on your scenario rate and combined with returns in a way that, after annualizing, slightly reduces the modeled real (inflation-adjusted) return — another conservative lean.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
`;
